                      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">The condition negatively affecting daily life, work or social relationships.</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: What causes panic attacks?
    giris: 'There is no single cause for panic attacks. Multiple factors often work together:'
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Biological factors
        metin: Hypersensitivity of the amygdala, imbalances in noradrenaline and serotonin, and genetic predisposition can all contribute to panic attacks.
      - baslik: Psychological factors
        metin: High levels of anxiety, a strong need for control, past traumatic experiences and negative thought patterns frequently accompany panic attacks.
      - baslik: Life stressors
        metin: Job loss, relationship difficulties, health worries, major life changes and chronic stress can all act as triggers.
      - baslik: Substance and medication effects
        metin: Excessive caffeine, nicotine withdrawal and certain medication side effects can also trigger panic attacks.
  - type: ozel
    html: |-
      <div>
                  <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl text-ink mb-4">What should you do during a panic attack?</h2>
                  <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">The techniques below may not stop an attack immediately, but they reduce its intensity and help it pass more quickly.</p>

                  <div class="space-y-5">
                    <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                      <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">1</div>
                      <div>
                        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink mb-1">Diaphragmatic breathing</p>
                        <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Breathe in through your nose for 4 counts, hold for 2 seconds, breathe out through your mouth for 6 counts. Slow breathing calms the nervous system and stops hyperventilation.</p>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                      <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">2</div>
                      <div>
                        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink mb-1">The 5-4-3-2-1 technique</p>
                        <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Name 5 things you can see, 4 things you can touch, 3 things you can hear, 2 things you can smell, 1 thing you can taste. This sensory exercise anchors you to the present moment and reduces feelings of unreality.</p>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                      <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">3</div>
                      <div>
                        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink mb-1">Remind yourself "this will pass"</p>
                        <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Panic attacks typically peak within 10–20 minutes and then subside. Reminding yourself that you are not in danger breaks the avoidance cycle.</p>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="flex gap-4 items-start">
                      <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-full bg-primary flex items-center justify-center text-white text-sm font-bold shrink-0">4</div>
                      <div>
                        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink mb-1">Do not flee the situation</p>
                        <p class="text-xs text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Leaving the situation brings short-term relief, but in the long run your fear of that place intensifies. Where possible, stay where you are and allow the attack to pass.</p>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: turuncu
    baslik: When should you see a professional?
    metin: If you are experiencing more than one panic attack a month, have started avoiding certain places out of fear of an attack, or your daily functioning has been disrupted, it is recommended that you consult a mental health professional. Panic disorder is highly treatable with Cognitive Behavioural Therapy.
